{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01389","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01389","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01389","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ulas Berk","familyName":"Karli","name":"Karli, Ulas Berk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyao","familyName":"Shangguan","name":"Shangguan, Ziyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tesca","familyName":"FItzgerald","name":"FItzgerald, Tesca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"INSIGHT: INference-time Sequence Introspection for Generating Help Triggers in Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T19:22:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:07:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-24T16:18:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:08:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/icra57385.2026.11697385","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models show strong generalization capabilities, yet they lack introspective mechanisms for anticipating failures and requesting help from a human supervisor. We present \\textbf{INSIGHT}, a learning framework for leveraging token-level uncertainty signals to predict when a VLA should request help. Using $π_0$-FAST as the underlying model, we extract per-token \\emph{entropy}, \\emph{log-probability}, and Dirichlet-based estimates of \\emph{aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty}, and train compact transformer classifiers to map these sequences to help triggers. We explore supervision regimes for strong or weak supervision, and extensively compare them across in-distribution and out-of-distribution tasks. Our results show a trade-off: strong labels enable models to capture fine-grained uncertainty dynamics for reliable help detection, while weak labels, though noisier, still support competitive introspection when training and evaluation are aligned, offering a scalable path when dense annotation is impractical. Crucially, we find that modeling the temporal evolution of token-level uncertainty signals with transformers provides far greater predictive power than static sequence-level scores. This study provides the first systematic evaluation of uncertainty-based introspection in VLAs, opening future avenues for active learning and for real-time error mitigation through selective human intervention."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01389","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-03T01:47:54Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:47:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:43:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Romero","name":"Romero, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ariana","familyName":"Bermudez","name":"Bermudez, Ariana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viacheslav","familyName":"Iablochnikov","name":"Iablochnikov, Viacheslav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Pizzati","name":"Pizzati, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Laptev","name":"Laptev, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Generate Rigid Body Interactions with Video Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:56:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:03:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-30T12:48:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:47:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-20T20:48:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-24T00:12:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-27T18:53:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-29T01:49:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent video generation models have achieved remarkable progress and are now deployed in film, social media production, and advertising. Beyond their creative potential, such models also hold promise as world simulators for robotics and embodied decision making. Despite strong advances, current approaches still struggle to generate physically plausible object interactions and lack object-level control mechanisms. To address these limitations, we introduce KineMask, an approach for video generation that enables realistic rigid body control, interactions, and effects. Given a single image and a specified object velocity, our method generates videos with inferred motions and future object interactions. We propose a two-stage training strategy that gradually removes future motion supervision via object masks. Using this strategy we train video diffusion models (VDMs) on synthetic scenes of simple interactions and demonstrate significant improvements and generalization to rigid body and hand-object interactions in real scenes. Furthermore, KineMask integrates low-level motion control with high-level textual conditioning via predicted scene descriptions, leading to support for synthesis of complex dynamical phenomena. Our experiments show that KineMask generalizes to different VDMs and achieves strong improvements over recent models of comparable size. Ablation studies further highlight the complementary roles of low- and high-level conditioning in VDMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:10:16Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:10:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T06:56:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01483","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01483","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01483","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamad Al","familyName":"Mdfaa","name":"Mdfaa, Mohamad Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Svetlana","familyName":"Lukina","name":"Lukina, Svetlana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timur","familyName":"Akhtyamov","name":"Akhtyamov, Timur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arthur","familyName":"Nigmatzyanov","name":"Nigmatzyanov, Arthur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitrii","familyName":"Nalberskii","name":"Nalberskii, Dmitrii","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Zagoruyko","name":"Zagoruyko, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gonzalo","familyName":"Ferrer","name":"Ferrer, Gonzalo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spatiotemporal Knowledge Graphs as Persistent Scene Memory for Embodied Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T21:53:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:12:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-24T11:41:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-25T00:53:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-20T15:53:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-21T01:45:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) demonstrate strong image-level scene understanding, but reasoning over long egocentric video remains costly: because VLMs maintain no persistent memory or explicit spatial representation, all sampled frames must be re-processed for every new query. We present VL-KnG, a training-free framework that constructs spatiotemporal knowledge graphs from monocular egocentric video, bridging fine-grained scene graphs and global topological graphs without 3D reconstruction. VL-KnG processes video in chunks, maintains persistent object identities via large language model (LLM)-based Spatiotemporal Object Association (STOA), and answers questions through Graph-Enhanced Retrieval (GER), which combines subgraph retrieval with visual grounding. Once constructed, the knowledge graph removes the need to revisit raw video at query time, decoupling query latency from video length. Across three embodied question answering benchmarks -- OpenEQA, NaVQA, and our newly introduced WalkieKnowledge -- VL-KnG achieves accuracy competitive with frontier VLMs while answering queries at substantially lower latency and with explainable, graph-grounded reasoning; it further surpasses prior persistent-representation baselines and open-weight VLMs in several settings. Deployment on a real robot demonstrates practical applicability, with query latency remaining stable as observation history grows. As a persistent, queryable scene memory, VL-KnG provides a concrete substrate for memory maintenance and knowledge updating in embodied AI agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01483","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:50:12Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:50:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T03:43:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01841","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01841","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01841","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giyeol","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Giyeol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sooyoung","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Sooyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihyong","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Jihyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Myungjoo","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Myungjoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanho","familyName":"Eom","name":"Eom, Chanho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leveraging Prior Knowledge of Diffusion Model for Person Search"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T09:36:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:36:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-14T08:41:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-15T00:36:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Person search aims to jointly perform person detection and re-identification by localizing and identifying a query person within a gallery of uncropped scene images. Existing methods predominantly utilize ImageNet pre-trained backbones, which may be suboptimal for capturing the complex spatial context and fine-grained identity cues necessary for person search. Moreover, they rely on a shared backbone feature for both person detection and re-identification, leading to suboptimal features due to conflicting optimization objectives. In this paper, we propose DiffPS (Diffusion Prior Knowledge for Person Search), a novel framework that leverages a pre-trained diffusion model while eliminating the optimization conflict between two sub-tasks. We analyze key properties of diffusion priors and propose three specialized modules: (i) Diffusion-Guided Region Proposal Network (DGRPN) for enhanced person localization, (ii) Multi-Scale Frequency Refinement Network (MSFRN) to mitigate shape bias, and (iii) Semantic-Adaptive Feature Aggregation Network (SFAN) to leverage text-aligned diffusion features. DiffPS sets a new state-of-the-art on CUHK-SYSU and PRW."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01841","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:59:15Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:59:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-15T02:16:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01328","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01328","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01328","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gert","familyName":"Aarts","name":"Aarts, Gert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diaa E.","familyName":"Habibi","name":"Habibi, Diaa E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingxiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lingxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Combining complex Langevin dynamics with score-based and energy-based diffusion models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Lattice (hep-lat)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T18:00:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-14T00:49:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/jhep12(2025)160","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Theories with a sign problem due to a complex action or Boltzmann weight can sometimes be numerically solved using a stochastic process in the complexified configuration space. However, the probability distribution effectively sampled by this complex Langevin process is not known a priori and notoriously hard to understand. In generative AI, diffusion models can learn distributions, or their log derivatives, from data. We explore the ability of diffusion models to learn the distributions sampled by a complex Langevin process, comparing score-based and energy-based diffusion models, and speculate about possible applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, many figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01328","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-03T01:46:23Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:46:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-14T03:57:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01642","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01642","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01642","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijun","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiafei","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Jiafei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoquan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Haoquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dieter","familyName":"Fox","name":"Fox, Dieter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ranjay","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Ranjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheston","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Cheston","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bihan","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Bihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FailSafe: Reasoning and Recovery from Failures in Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T03:48:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:23:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T07:38:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:27:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T05:16:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:22:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-07T04:25:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-08T00:21:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in robotic manipulation have integrated low-level robotic control into Vision-Language Models (VLMs), extending them into Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models. Although state-of-the-art VLAs achieve strong performance in downstream robotic applications, supported by large-scale crowd-sourced robot training data, they still inevitably encounter failures during execution. Enabling robots to reason and recover from unpredictable and abrupt failures remains a critical challenge. Existing robotic manipulation datasets, collected in either simulation or the real world, primarily provide only ground-truth trajectories, leaving robots unable to recover once failures occur. Moreover, the few datasets that address failure detection typically offer only textual explanations, which are difficult to utilize directly in VLA models. To address this gap, we introduce FailSafe, a novel failure generation and recovery system that automatically produces diverse failure cases paired with executable recovery actions. FailSafe can be easily adapted to a wide range of manipulation tasks in simulators with motion planning support, enabling scalable creation of failure-action data. To demonstrate its effectiveness, we fine-tune LLaVA-OneVision-7B (LLaVA-OV-7B) to build FailSafe-VLM. Experimental results show that FailSafe-VLM successfully helps robotic arms detect and recover from potential failures, improving the performance of three state-of-the-art VLA models (Pi-0-FAST, OpenVLA, OpenVLA-OFT) by up to 22.6% on average across several tasks in ManiSkill. Furthermore, FailSafe-VLM could generalize across different spatial configurations, camera viewpoints, object and robotic embodiments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"IROS 2026. Project Page: https://jimntu.github.io/FailSafe"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01642","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:54:12Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:54:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-08T02:09:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02208","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02208","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02208","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirreza","familyName":"Tanevardi","name":"Tanevardi, Amirreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pooria Abbas Rad","familyName":"Moghadam","name":"Moghadam, Pooria Abbas Rad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyed Mohammad","familyName":"Eshtehardian","name":"Eshtehardian, Seyed Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sajjad","familyName":"Amini","name":"Amini, Sajjad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Babak","familyName":"Khalaj","name":"Khalaj, Babak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MACS: Measurement-Aware Consistency Sampling for Inverse Problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:53:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:59:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-03T06:40:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:26:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-04T07:02:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:08:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have emerged as powerful generative priors for solving inverse imaging problems. However, their practical deployment is hindered by the substantial computational cost of slow, multi-step sampling. Although Consistency Models (CMs) address this limitation by enabling high-quality generation in only one or a few steps, their direct application to inverse problems has remained largely unexplored. This paper introduces a modified consistency sampling framework specifically designed for inverse problems. The proposed approach regulates the sampler's stochasticity through a measurement-consistency mechanism that leverages the degradation operator, thereby enforcing fidelity to the observed data while preserving the computational efficiency of consistency-based generation. Comprehensive experiments on the Fashion-MNIST and LSUN Bedroom datasets demonstrate consistent improvements across both perceptual and pixel-level metrics, including the Fréchet Inception Distance (FID), Kernel Inception Distance (KID), peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR), and structural similarity index measure (SSIM), compared with baseline consistency and diffusion-based sampling methods. The proposed method achieves competitive or superior reconstruction quality with only a small number of sampling steps."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02208","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:08:23Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:08:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T04:58:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01444","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01444","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01444","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dian","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Dian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runpeng","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Runpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongxia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zongxia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenwen","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhenwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitao","familyName":"Mi","name":"Mi, Haitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pratap","familyName":"Tokekar","name":"Tokekar, Pratap","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dual-Uncertainty Guided Policy Learning for Multimodal Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T20:32:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:09:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-15T17:51:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-16T01:57:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T19:14:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:07:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) has advanced reasoning capabilities in multimodal large language models. However, existing methods typically treat visual inputs as deterministic, overlooking the perceptual ambiguity inherent to the visual modality. Consequently, they fail to distinguish whether a model's uncertainty stems from complex reasoning or ambiguous perception, preventing the targeted allocation of exploration or learning signals. To address this gap, we introduce \\textbf{DUPL}, a dual-uncertainty guided policy learning approach for multimodal RLVR that quantifies and leverages both perceptual uncertainty (via symmetric KL divergence) and output uncertainty (via policy entropy) to guide policy updates. By establishing an uncertainty-driven feedback loop and employing a dynamic branch prioritization mechanism, DUPL recalibrates the policy advantage to focus learning on states with high perceptual or decisional ambiguity, enabling effective targeted exploration beyond passive data augmentation. Evaluated on diverse multimodal reasoning benchmarks spanning mathematical and general domains, DUPL achieves solid gains. It improves Qwen2.5-VL accuracy by up to $\\textbf{12.3%}$ (3B) and $\\textbf{7.9%}$ (7B), and Qwen3-VL-Instruct by up to $\\textbf{10.7%}$ (4B) and $\\textbf{12.4%}$ (8B), consistently outperforming GRPO, while seamlessly generalizing to alternative algorithms (DAPO, $\\textbf{+6.5%}$ avg) and architectures (LLaVA-OneVision-1.5, $\\textbf{+4.7%}$ avg). These results demonstrate that DUPL is an effective and generalizable approach for multimodal RLVR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01444","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:49:15Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:49:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-16T04:06:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01711","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01711","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01711","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taeyoung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Taeyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jimin","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Myungkyu","familyName":"Koo","name":"Koo, Myungkyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyoung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dongyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyungmin","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Kyungmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Changyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Younggyo","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Younggyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwoo","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Jinwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Contrastive Representation Regularization for Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T06:41:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:28:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T07:50:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:38:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-28T04:47:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-29T00:30:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-31T14:16:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:13:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have shown strong capabilities in robot manipulation by leveraging rich representations from pre-trained Vision-Language Models (VLMs). However, their representations arguably remain suboptimal, lacking sensitivity to robotic signals such as control actions and proprioceptive information. To address the issue, we introduce Robot State-aware Contrastive Loss (RS-CL), a simple and effective representation regularization for VLA models, designed to bridge the gap between VLM representations and robotic signals. In particular, RS-CL aligns the representations more closely with the robot's proprioceptive states by using relative distances between the states as soft supervision. Complementing the original action prediction objective, RS-CL enhances control-relevant representation learning, while being lightweight and fully compatible with standard VLA training pipelines. Our empirical results demonstrate that RS-CL substantially improves the performance of state-of-the-art VLA models; it pushes the prior art to 69.7% achieving the state-of-the-art performance on the RoboCasa-Kitchen benchmark, and boosts success rates from 45.0% to 58.3% on challenging real-robot manipulation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01711","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:56:02Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:56:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01384","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01384","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01384","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaeyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jaeyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunggeun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seunggeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taekyun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Taekyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Z.","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, David Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeji","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyeji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sham","familyName":"Kakade","name":"Kakade, Sham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sitan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Sitan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for Provable Self-Correction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T19:15:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:07:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-07T04:01:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:18:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-19T06:19:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-22T01:20:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-22T19:48:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-26T00:08:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A natural desideratum for generative models is self-correction--detecting and revising low-quality tokens at inference. While Masked Diffusion Models (MDMs) have emerged as a promising approach for generative modeling in discrete spaces, their capacity for self-correction remains poorly understood. Prior attempts to incorporate self-correction into MDMs either require overhauling MDM architectures/training or rely on imprecise proxies for token quality, limiting their applicability. Motivated by this, we introduce PRISM--Plug-in Remasking for Inference-time Self-correction of Masked Diffusions--a lightweight, model-agnostic approach that applies to any pretrained MDM. Theoretically, PRISM defines a self-correction loss that provably learns per-token quality scores, without RL or a verifier. These quality scores are computed in the same forward pass with MDM and used to detect low-quality tokens. Empirically, PRISM advances MDM inference across domains and scales: Sudoku; unconditional text (170M); and code with LLaDA (8B)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Authorship statement: Jaeyeon Kim and Seunggeun Kim contributed equally, and Taekyun Lee is also a co first author"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01384","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:47:47Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:47:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:04:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02307","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02307","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02307","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruozhen","familyName":"He","name":"He, Ruozhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moayed","familyName":"Haji-Ali","name":"Haji-Ali, Moayed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vicente","familyName":"Ordonez","name":"Ordonez, Vicente","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NoiseShift: Resolution-Aware Noise Recalibration for Better Low-Resolution Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:59:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:04:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-14T21:42:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-18T00:11:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image diffusion models often degrade when sampled at resolutions outside the final training resolution set. Prior work has largely emphasized higher resolution generation, enabling pretrained diffusion models to extrapolate beyond the resolutions seen during training. In this work, we instead target lower-resolution generation, performing inference at reduced resolution to significantly cut computational cost. We show that network conditioning of the noise level induces a train-test mismatch that directly degrades low-resolution generation: the same scheduled noise level can correspond to a different perceptual corruption level at lower resolutions, mis-calibrating the denoiser timestep and noise embedding. To this end, we propose NoiseShift, a training-free recalibration method that keeps the original noise sampling schedule unchanged and instead re-indexes the noise conditioning of the denoiser to restore local forward-reverse consistency. Using a lightweight coarse-to-fine calibration on a small set of image-text pairs, NoiseShift learns a resolution-specific mapping from scheduler noise to conditioning noise, reducing train-test mismatch and improving lower-resolution generation quality. When NoiseShift is applied to Stable Diffusion 3 (SD3), Stable Diffusion 3.5 (SD3.5), and Flux-Dev, generation quality at low resolutions improves consistently. Particularly, SD3 generation at 128x128 resolution gets an improved FID score from 203 to 171, and SD3.5 gets an improved FID score from 310 to 277 on LAION-COCO. Even Flux-Dev which already implements a complementary time-shifting strategy gets a modest boost from NoiseShift with an improved FID score from 120 to 113 at 64x64 resolution. More importantly, NoiseShift achieves such improvements with minimal implementation changes and no additional inference overhead."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02307","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:10:49Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:10:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-18T02:13:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02043","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02043","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02043","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahil Bhandary","familyName":"Karnoor","name":"Karnoor, Sahil Bhandary","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Romit Roy","familyName":"Choudhury","name":"Choudhury, Romit Roy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Zero-shot Human Pose Estimation using Diffusion-based Inverse solvers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T14:16:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:49:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:19:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:23:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pose estimation refers to tracking a human's full body posture, including their head, torso, arms, and legs. The problem is challenging in practical settings where the number of body sensors are limited. Past work has shown promising results using conditional diffusion models, where the pose prediction is conditioned on both measurements from the sensors. Unfortunately, nearly all these approaches generalize poorly across users, primarly because location measurements are highly influenced by the body size of the user. In this paper, we formulate pose estimation as an inverse problem and design an algorithm capable of zero-shot generalization. Our idea utilizes a pre-trained diffusion model and conditions it on rotational measurements alone; the priors from this model are then guided by a likelihood term, derived from the measured locations. Thus, given any user, our proposed InPose method generatively estimates the highly likely sequence of poses that best explains the sparse on-body measurements."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a Conference Paper at The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02043","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:04:17Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:04:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02107","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02107","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02107","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Klaus-Rudolf","familyName":"Kladny","name":"Kladny, Klaus-Rudolf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernhard","familyName":"Schölkopf","name":"Schölkopf, Bernhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Muehlebach","name":"Muehlebach, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PENEX: AdaBoost-Inspired Neural Network Regularization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T15:13:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:52:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-06T17:51:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:49:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T12:09:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:48:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T09:50:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:53:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AdaBoost sequentially fits so-called weak learners to minimize an exponential loss, which penalizes misclassified data points more severely than other loss functions like cross-entropy. Paradoxically, AdaBoost generalizes well in practice as the number of weak learners grows. In the present work, we introduce Penalized Exponential Loss (PENEX), a new formulation of the multi-class exponential loss that is theoretically grounded and, in contrast to the existing formulation, amenable to optimization via first-order methods, making it a practical objective for training neural networks. We demonstrate that PENEX effectively increases margins of data points, which can be translated into a generalization bound. Empirically, across computer vision and language tasks, PENEX improves neural network generalization in low-data regimes, matching and in some settings outperforming established regularizers at comparable computational cost. Our results highlight the potential of the exponential loss beyond its application in AdaBoost."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02107","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:05:53Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:05:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhengyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiezhang","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jiezhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yulun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FideDiff: Efficient Diffusion Model for High-Fidelity Image Motion Deblurring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T03:44:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:23:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-21T07:45:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T00:33:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-06T06:42:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-07T00:27:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in image motion deblurring, driven by CNNs and transformers, have made significant progress. Large-scale pre-trained diffusion models, which are rich in real-world modeling, have shown great promise for high-quality image restoration tasks such as deblurring, demonstrating stronger generative capabilities than CNN and transformer-based methods. However, challenges such as unbearable inference time and compromised fidelity still limit the full potential of the diffusion models. To address this, we introduce FideDiff, a novel single-step diffusion model designed for high-fidelity deblurring. We reformulate motion deblurring as a diffusion-like process where each timestep represents a progressively blurred image, and we train a consistency model that aligns all timesteps to the same clean image. By reconstructing training data with matched blur trajectories, the model learns temporal consistency, enabling accurate one-step deblurring. We further enhance model performance by integrating Kernel ControlNet for blur kernel estimation and introducing adaptive timestep prediction. Our model achieves superior performance on full-reference metrics, surpassing previous diffusion-based methods and matching the performance of other state-of-the-art models. FideDiff offers a new direction for applying pre-trained diffusion models to high-fidelity image restoration tasks, establishing a robust baseline for further advancing diffusion models in real-world industrial applications. Our dataset and code will be available at https://github.com/xyLiu339/FideDiff."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026. Code is available at https://github.com/xyLiu339/FideDiff"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:54:10Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:54:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-07T02:18:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02001","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02001","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02001","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanaka","familyName":"Hosokawa","name":"Hosokawa, Nanaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryo","familyName":"Takahashi","name":"Takahashi, Ryo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomoya","familyName":"Kitano","name":"Kitano, Tomoya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukihiro","familyName":"Iida","name":"Iida, Yukihiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chisako","familyName":"Muramatsu","name":"Muramatsu, Chisako","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatsuro","familyName":"Hayashi","name":"Hayashi, Tatsuro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuta","familyName":"Seino","name":"Seino, Yuta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangrong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiangrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takeshi","familyName":"Hara","name":"Hara, Takeshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akitoshi","familyName":"Katsumata","name":"Katsumata, Akitoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroshi","familyName":"Fujita","name":"Fujita, Hiroshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generating Findings for Jaw Cysts in Dental Panoramic Radiographs Using a GPT-Based VLM: A Preliminary Study on Building a Two-Stage Self-Correction Loop with Structured Output (SLSO) Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T13:22:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:46:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-06T14:02:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:35:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-17T00:52:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-18T01:14:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-24T00:47:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-25T00:20:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-30T10:54:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-15T00:44:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-20T01:16:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:31:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3390/diagnostics16071096","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) such as GPT (Generative Pre-Trained Transformer) have shown potential for medical image interpretation; however, challenges remain in generating reliable radiological findings in clinical practice, as exemplified by dental pathologies. This study proposes a Self-correction Loop with Structured Output (SLSO) framework as an integrated processing methodology to enhance the accuracy and reliability of AI-generated findings for jaw cysts in dental panoramic radiographs. Dental panoramic radiographs with jaw cysts were used to implement a 10-step integrated processing framework incorporating image analysis, structured data generation, tooth number extraction, consistency checking, and iterative regeneration. The framework functioned as an external validation mechanism for GPT outputs. Performance was compared against the conventional Chain-of-Thought (CoT) method across seven evaluation items: transparency, internal structure, borders, root resorption, tooth movement, relationships with other structures, and tooth number. The SLSO framework improved output accuracy for multiple items compared to the CoT method, with the most notable improvements observed in tooth number identification, tooth movement detection, and root resorption assessment. In successful cases, consistently structured outputs were achieved after up to five regenerations. The framework enforced explicit negative finding descriptions and suppressed hallucinations, although accurate identification of extensive lesions spanning multiple teeth remained limited. This investigation established the feasibility of the proposed integrated processing methodology and provided a foundation for future validation studies with larger, more diverse datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Revised manuscript; supplementary materials added. Published in Diagnostics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02001","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-03T02:03:14Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:03:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:49:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01608","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01608","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01608","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roman","familyName":"Jacome","name":"Jacome, Roman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Romario","familyName":"Gualdrón-Hurtado","name":"Gualdrón-Hurtado, Romario","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leon","familyName":"Suarez","name":"Suarez, Leon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Arguello","name":"Arguello, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NPN: Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space for Imaging Inverse Problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:45:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:21:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T22:21:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:05:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Imaging inverse problems aim to recover high-dimensional signals from undersampled, noisy measurements, a fundamentally ill-posed task with infinite solutions in the null-space of the sensing operator. To resolve this ambiguity, prior information is typically incorporated through handcrafted regularizers or learned models that constrain the solution space. However, these priors typically ignore the task-specific structure of that null-space. In this work, we propose Non-Linear Projections of the Null-Space (NPN), a novel class of regularization that, instead of enforcing structural constraints in the image domain, promotes solutions that lie in a low-dimensional projection of the sensing matrix's null-space with a neural network. Our approach has two key advantages: (1) Interpretability: by focusing on the structure of the null-space, we design sensing-matrix-specific priors that capture information orthogonal to the signal components that are fundamentally blind to the sensing process. (2) Flexibility: NPN is adaptable to various inverse problems, compatible with existing reconstruction frameworks, and complementary to conventional image-domain priors. We provide theoretical guarantees on convergence and reconstruction accuracy when used within plug-and-play methods. Empirical results across diverse sensing matrices demonstrate that NPN priors consistently enhance reconstruction fidelity in various imaging inverse problems, such as compressive sensing, deblurring, super-resolution, computed tomography, and magnetic resonance imaging, with plug-and-play methods, unrolling networks, deep image prior, and diffusion models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 12 tables, 10 figures. Accepted to NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01608","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:53:22Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:53:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02226","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02226","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02226","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shira","familyName":"Schiber","name":"Schiber, Shira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ofir","familyName":"Lindenbaum","name":"Lindenbaum, Ofir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Idan","familyName":"Schwartz","name":"Schwartz, Idan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TempoControl: Temporal Attention Guidance for Text-to-Video Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:13:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:00:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-05T13:22:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:36:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-01T14:48:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-02T00:59:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in generative video models have enabled the creation of high-quality videos based on natural language prompts. However, these models frequently lack fine-grained temporal control, meaning they do not allow users to specify when particular visual elements should appear within a generated sequence. In this work, we introduce TempoControl, a method that allows for temporal alignment of visual concepts during inference, without requiring retraining or additional supervision. TempoControl utilizes cross-attention maps, a key component of text-to-video diffusion models, to guide the timing of concepts through a novel optimization approach. Our method steers attention using three complementary principles: aligning its temporal pattern with a control signal (correlation), adjusting its strength where visibility is required (magnitude), and preserving semantic consistency (entropy). TempoControl provides precise temporal control while maintaining high video quality and diversity. We demonstrate its effectiveness across various applications, including temporal reordering of single and multiple objects, action timing, and audio-aligned video generation. Project page: https://shira-schiber.github.io/TempoControl/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted CVPR'26"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02226","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:08:50Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:08:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-02T02:28:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02315","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02315","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02315","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric Tillmann","familyName":"Bill","name":"Bill, Eric Tillmann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enis","familyName":"Simsar","name":"Simsar, Enis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Hofmann","name":"Hofmann, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FOCUS: Optimal Control for Multi-Entity World Modeling in Text-to-Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:59:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:04:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-25T16:15:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T01:07:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image (T2I) models excel on single-entity prompts but struggle with multi-entity scenes, often exhibiting attribute leakage, identity entanglement, and subject omissions. We present a principled theoretical framework that steers sampling toward multi-subject fidelity by casting flow matching (FM) as stochastic optimal control (SOC), yielding a single hyperparameter controlled trade-off between fidelity and object-centric state separation / binding consistency. Within this framework, we derive two architecture-agnostic algorithms: (i) a training-free test-time controller that perturbs the base velocity with a single-pass update, and (ii) Adjoint Matching, a lightweight fine-tuning rule that regresses a control network to a backward adjoint signal. The same formulation unifies prior attention heuristics, extends to diffusion models via a flow--diffusion correspondence, and provides the first fine-tuning route explicitly designed for multi-subject fidelity. In addition, we also introduce FOCUS (Flow Optimal Control for Unentangled Subjects), a probabilistic attention-binding objective compatible with both algorithms. Empirically, on Stable Diffusion 3.5 and FLUX.1, both algorithms consistently improve multi-subject alignment while maintaining base-model style; test-time control runs efficiently on commodity GPUs, and fine-tuned models generalize to unseen prompts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://ericbill21.github.io/FOCUS/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02315","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:11:00Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:11:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:29:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yule","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yule","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengrui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chengrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anqi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Anqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncovering Semantic Selectivity of Latent Groups in Higher Visual Cortex with Mutual Information-Guided Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:33:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:58:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-10T22:26:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-12T00:12:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding how neural populations in higher visual areas encode object-centered visual information remains a central challenge in computational neuroscience. Prior works have investigated representational alignment between artificial neural networks and the visual cortex. Nevertheless, these findings are indirect and offer limited insights to the structure of neural populations themselves. Similarly, decoding-based methods have quantified semantic features from neural populations but have not uncovered their underlying organizations. This leaves open a scientific question: \"how feature-specific visual information is distributed across neural populations in higher visual areas, and whether it is organized into structured, semantically meaningful subspaces.\" To tackle this problem, we present MIG-Vis, a method that leverages the generative power of diffusion models to visualize and validate the visual-semantic attributes encoded in neural latent subspaces. Our method first uses a variational autoencoder to infer a group-wise disentangled neural latent subspace from neural populations. Subsequently, we propose a mutual information (MI)-guided diffusion synthesis procedure to visualize the specific visual-semantic features encoded by each latent group. We validate MIG-Vis on multi-session neural spiking datasets from the inferior temporal (IT) cortex of two macaques. The synthesized results demonstrate that our method identifies neural latent groups with clear semantic selectivity to diverse visual features, including object pose, inter-category transformations, and intra-class content. These findings provide direct, interpretable evidence of structured semantic representation in the higher visual cortex and advance our understanding of its encoding principles."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:07:44Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:07:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-12T02:25:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02253","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02253","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02253","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shilin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Shilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuli","familyName":"Leng","name":"Leng, Shuli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaocong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shaocong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuming","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Zhuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinlei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xinlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adams Wai-Kin","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Adams Wai-Kin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DragFlow: Unleashing DiT Priors with Region Based Supervision for Drag Editing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:39:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:01:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T17:58:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T01:04:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T15:34:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:16:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Drag-based image editing has long suffered from distortions in the target region, largely because the priors of earlier base models, Stable Diffusion, are insufficient to project optimized latents back onto the natural image manifold. With the shift from UNet-based DDPMs to more scalable DiT with flow matching (e.g., SD3.5, FLUX), generative priors have become significantly stronger, enabling advances across diverse editing tasks. However, drag-based editing has yet to benefit from these stronger priors. This work proposes the first framework to effectively harness FLUX's rich prior for drag-based editing, dubbed DragFlow, achieving substantial gains over baselines. We first show that directly applying point-based drag editing to DiTs performs poorly: unlike the highly compressed features of UNets, DiT features are insufficiently structured to provide reliable guidance for point-wise motion supervision. To overcome this limitation, DragFlow introduces a region-based editing paradigm, where affine transformations enable richer and more consistent feature supervision. Additionally, we integrate pretrained open-domain personalization adapters (e.g., IP-Adapter) to enhance subject consistency, while preserving background fidelity through gradient mask-based hard constraints. Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) are further employed to resolve task ambiguities. For evaluation, we curate a novel Region-based Dragging benchmark (ReD Bench) featuring region-level dragging instructions. Extensive experiments on DragBench-DR and ReD Bench show that DragFlow surpasses both point-based and region-based baselines, setting a new state-of-the-art in drag-based image editing. Code and dataset are available at https://github.com/Edennnnnnnnnn/DragFlow."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02253","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:09:30Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:09:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01339","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01339","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01339","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Spagnoletti","name":"Spagnoletti, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrés","familyName":"Almansa","name":"Almansa, Andrés","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcelo","familyName":"Pereyra","name":"Pereyra, Marcelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LVTINO: LAtent Video consisTency INverse sOlver for High Definition Video Restoration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T18:10:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:04:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T16:12:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T03:19:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computational imaging methods increasingly rely on powerful generative diffusion models to tackle challenging image restoration tasks. In particular, state-of-the-art zero-shot image inverse solvers leverage distilled text-to-image latent diffusion models (LDMs) to achieve unprecedented accuracy and perceptual quality with high computational efficiency. However, extending these advances to high-definition video restoration remains a significant challenge, due to the need to recover fine spatial detail while capturing subtle temporal dependencies. Consequently, methods that naively apply image-based LDM priors on a frame-by-frame basis often result in temporally inconsistent reconstructions. We address this challenge by leveraging recent advances in Video Consistency Models (VCMs), which distill video latent diffusion models into fast generators that explicitly capture temporal causality. Building on this foundation, we propose LVTINO, the first zero-shot or plug-and-play inverse solver for high definition video restoration with priors encoded by VCMs. Our conditioning mechanism bypasses the need for automatic differentiation and achieves state-of-the-art video reconstruction quality with only a few neural function evaluations, while ensuring strong measurement consistency and smooth temporal transitions across frames. Extensive experiments on a diverse set of video inverse problems show significant perceptual improvements over current state-of-the-art methods that apply image LDMs frame by frame, establishing a new benchmark in both reconstruction fidelity and computational efficiency. The code is available on GitHub."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, 16 figures. The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations, ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01339","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:46:40Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:46:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02240","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02240","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02240","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicheng","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Sicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"Tuo","name":"Tuo, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingdong","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Lingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianke","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jianke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RewardMap: Tackling Sparse Rewards in Fine-grained Visual Reasoning via Multi-Stage Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:29:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:01:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-21T17:38:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:33:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-grained visual reasoning remains a core challenge for multimodal large language models (MLLMs). The recently introduced ReasonMap highlights this gap by showing that even advanced MLLMs struggle with spatial reasoning in structured and information-rich settings such as transit maps, a task of clear practical and scientific importance. However, standard reinforcement learning (RL) on such tasks is impeded by sparse rewards and unstable optimization. To address this, we first construct ReasonMap-Plus, an extended dataset that introduces dense reward signals through Visual Question Answering (VQA) tasks, enabling effective cold-start training of fine-grained visual understanding skills. Next, we propose RewardMap, a multi-stage RL framework designed to improve both visual understanding and reasoning capabilities of MLLMs. RewardMap incorporates two key designs. First, we introduce a difficulty-aware reward design that incorporates detail rewards, directly tackling the sparse rewards while providing richer supervision. Second, we propose a multi-stage RL scheme that bootstraps training from simple perception to complex reasoning tasks, offering a more effective cold-start strategy than conventional Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT). Experiments on ReasonMap and ReasonMap-Plus demonstrate that each component of RewardMap contributes to consistent performance gains, while their combination yields the best results. Moreover, models trained with RewardMap achieve an average improvement of 3.47% across 6 benchmarks spanning spatial reasoning, fine-grained visual reasoning, and general tasks beyond transit maps, underscoring enhanced visual understanding and reasoning capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026, website: https://fscdc.github.io/RewardMap/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02240","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:09:10Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:09:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02186","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02186","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02186","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Bin","name":"Bin, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Guoqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng Tao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Heng Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GeoPurify: A Data-Efficient Geometric Distillation Framework for Open-Vocabulary 3D Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:37:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:58:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-11T15:41:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-12T01:56:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent attempts to transfer features from 2D Vision-Language Models (VLMs) to 3D semantic segmentation expose a persistent trade-off. Directly projecting 2D features into 3D yields noisy and fragmented predictions, whereas enforcing geometric coherence necessitates costly training pipelines and large-scale annotated 3D data. We argue that this limitation stems from the dominant segmentation-and-matching paradigm, which fails to reconcile 2D semantics with 3D geometric structure. The geometric cues are not eliminated during the 2D-to-3D transfer but remain latent within the noisy and view-aggregated features. To exploit this property, we propose GeoPurify that applies a small Student Affinity Network to purify 2D VLM-generated 3D point features using geometric priors distilled from a 3D self-supervised teacher model. During inference, we devise a Geometry-Guided Pooling module to further denoise the point cloud and ensure the semantic and structural consistency. Benefiting from latent geometric information and the learned affinity network, GeoPurify effectively mitigates the trade-off and achieves superior data efficiency. Extensive experiments on major 3D benchmarks demonstrate that GeoPurify achieves or surpasses state-of-the-art performance while utilizing only about 1.5% of the training data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026. Code available at: https://github.com/tj12323/GeoPurify"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02186","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:07:50Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:07:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-12T03:21:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01304","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01304","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01304","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiting","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xikun","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Xikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukun","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuchen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qiuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Huaian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanli","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Wanli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic Jigsaw Interaction Learning for Enhancing Visual Perception and Reasoning in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:58:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:02:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-10T15:16:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-11T01:59:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-11T16:48:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-12T02:00:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although current large Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have advanced in multimodal understanding and reasoning, their fundamental perceptual and reasoning abilities remain limited. Specifically, even on simple jigsaw tasks, existing VLMs perform near randomly, revealing deficiencies in core perception and reasoning capabilities. While high-quality vision-language data can enhance these capabilities, its scarcity and limited scalability impose significant constraints. To address this, we propose AGILE, an Agentic jiGsaw Interaction Learning for Enhancing visual perception and reasoning in VLMs. AGILE formulates jigsaw solving as an interactive process, enabling the model to progressively engage with the environment. At each step, the model generates executable code to perform an action based on the current state, while the environment provides fine-grained visual feedback to guide task completion. Through this iterative cycle of observation and interaction, the model incrementally improves its perceptual and reasoning capabilities via exploration and feedback. Experimental results show that AGILE not only substantially boosts performance on jigsaw tasks of varying complexity (e.g., increasing accuracy from 9.5% to 82.8% under the 2 $\\times$ 2 setting) but also demonstrates strong generalization across 9 general vision tasks, achieving an average improvement of 3.1%. These results indicate notable enhancements in both perceptual and reasoning abilities. This work opens a new avenue for advancing reasoning and generalization in multimodal models and provides an efficient, scalable solution to the scarcity of multimodal reinforcement learning data. The code and datasets is available at https://github.com/yuzeng0-0/AGILE ."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01304","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:45:47Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:45:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-12T03:20:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02295","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02295","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02295","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enxin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Enxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shusheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shusheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Armand","name":"Armand, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojun","familyName":"Shan","name":"Shan, Xiaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianwen","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Jianwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuowen","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Zhuowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VideoNSA: Native Sparse Attention Scales Video Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:58:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:03:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T06:16:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:30:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video understanding in multimodal language models remains limited by context length: models often miss key transition frames and struggle to maintain coherence across long time scales. To address this, we adapt Native Sparse Attention (NSA) to video-language models. Our method, VideoNSA, adapts Qwen2.5-VL through end-to-end training on a 216K video instruction dataset. We employ a hardware-aware hybrid approach to attention, preserving dense attention for text, while employing NSA for video. Compared to token-compression and training-free sparse baselines, VideoNSA achieves improved performance on long-video understanding, temporal reasoning, and spatial benchmarks. Further ablation analysis reveals four key findings: (1) reliable scaling to 128K tokens; (2) an optimal global-local attention allocation at a fixed budget; (3) task-dependent branch usage patterns; and (4) the learnable combined sparse attention help induce dynamic attention sinks. Project Page: https://enxinsong.com/VideoNSA-web/, Code: https://github.com/Espere-1119-Song/VideoNSA"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02295","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:10:31Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:10:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02291","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02291","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02291","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Litu","familyName":"Rout","name":"Rout, Litu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Lugmayr","name":"Lugmayr, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasamin","familyName":"Jafarian","name":"Jafarian, Yasamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srivatsan","familyName":"Varadharajan","name":"Varadharajan, Srivatsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Constantine","familyName":"Caramanis","name":"Caramanis, Constantine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjay","familyName":"Shakkottai","name":"Shakkottai, Sanjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ira","familyName":"Kemelmacher-Shlizerman","name":"Kemelmacher-Shlizerman, Ira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Anchoring for Discrete Diffusion Posterior Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:58:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:03:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T03:35:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:22:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While continuous diffusion models have achieved remarkable success, discrete diffusion offers a unified framework for jointly modeling text and images. Beyond unification, discrete diffusion provides faster inference, finer control, and principled training-free guidance, making it well-suited for posterior sampling. Existing approaches to posterior sampling using discrete diffusion face severe challenges: derivative-free guidance yields sparse signals, continuous relaxations limit applicability, and split Gibbs samplers suffer from the curse of dimensionality. To overcome these limitations, we introduce Anchored Posterior Sampling (APS), built on two key innovations: quantized expectation for gradient-like guidance in discrete embedding space, and anchored remasking for adaptive decoding. APS achieves state-of-the-art performance among discrete diffusion samplers on both linear and nonlinear inverse problems across the standard image benchmarks. We demonstrate the generality of APS through training-free stylization and text-guided editing. We further apply APS to a large-scale diffusion language model, showing consistent improvement in question answering."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02291","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:10:26Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:10:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02081","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02081","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02081","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingtao","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Jingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shihua","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-Tuning Flow Matching via Maximum Likelihood Estimation of Reconstructions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T14:49:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:51:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-29T14:05:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:59:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Flow Matching (FM) models achieve remarkable results in generative tasks. Building upon diffusion models, FM's simulation-free training paradigm enables simplicity and efficiency but introduces a train-inference gap: model outputs cannot be assessed during training. Moreover, the straight flow assumption suffers from some inherent limitations. To address this, we propose to fine-tune FM via Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) of reconstructions -- enabled by FM's smooth ODE formulation, unlike the stochastic differential equations (SDEs) in diffusion models. We first theoretically analyze the relationship between training loss and inference error in FM under numerical precision constraints. We then propose an easy-to-implement fine-tuning framework based on MLE of reconstructions, with flexibility for sophisticated extensions. Building on this, we incorporate a generalized artificial viscosity term that enhances flow stability and robustness, accompanied by a direct parameterization method and rigorous theoretical guarantees. Experiments demonstrate our method's effectiveness across diverse settings: a toy example provides mechanistic insights into the fine-tuning process, while large-scale evaluations on meteorological forecasting and robotic manipulation policies validate reliable performance improvements."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02081","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:05:14Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:05:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-30T03:41:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02212","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02212","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02212","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawen","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Dawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenpin","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Wenpin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan","familyName":"Kallus","name":"Kallus, Nathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiFFPO: Training Diffusion LLMs to Reason Fast and Furious via Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:57:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:59:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-10T10:25:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:23:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose DiFFPO, Diffusion Fast and Furious Policy Optimization, a unified framework for training masked diffusion large language models (dLLMs) to reason not only better (furious), but also faster via reinforcement learning (RL). We first unify the existing baseline approach such as d1 by proposing to train surrogate policies via off-policy RL, whose likelihood is much more tractable as an approximation to the true dLLM policy. This naturally motivates a more accurate and informative two-stage likelihood approximation combined with importance sampling correction, which leads to generalized RL algorithms with better sample efficiency and superior task performance. Second, we propose a new direction of joint training efficient samplers/controllers of dLLMs policy. Via RL, we incentivize dLLMs' natural multi-token prediction capabilities by letting the model learn to adaptively allocate an inference threshold for each prompt. By jointly training the sampler, we yield better accuracies with lower number of function evaluations (NFEs) compared to training the model only, obtaining the best performance in improving the Pareto frontier of the inference-time compute of dLLMs. We showcase the effectiveness of our pipeline by training open source large diffusion language models over benchmark math and planning tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02212","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:08:29Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:08:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:26:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02262","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02262","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02262","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Guangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Archit","familyName":"Singhal","name":"Singhal, Archit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Burak","familyName":"Uzkent","name":"Uzkent, Burak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mubarak","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Mubarak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Garin","familyName":"Kessler","name":"Kessler, Garin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Frames to Clips: Training-free Adaptive Key Clip Selection for Long-Form Video Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:43:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:02:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-18T06:01:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-19T01:26:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video Large Language Models (VLMs) have achieved strong performance on various vision-language tasks, yet their practical use is limited by the massive number of visual tokens produced from raw video frames, which quickly exhausts the model's context window. Existing solutions mitigate this issue by selecting a sparse set of frames, but such frame-wise selection discards essential temporal dynamics in long-form videos, leading to suboptimal reasoning about motion and event continuity. In this work, we systematically examine the role of temporal information and show that extending selection from isolated key frames to temporally coherent key clips improves video understanding. To maintain a fixed computational budget while accommodating the larger token footprint of clips, we introduce frame resolution as a controllable factor in frame selection, enabling a trade-off between spatial resolution and clip length. Building on this idea, we propose an adaptive clip length module that dynamically balances these factors to ensure a constant token count per video. Experiments on three long-form video benchmarks demonstrate that our training-free approach, F2C, outperforms uniform sampling by up to 8.1%, 5.6%, and 10.3% on Video-MME, LongVideoBench, and MLVU, respectively. These results highlight the importance of preserving temporal coherence in frame selection and provide a practical pathway for scaling VLMs to real-world video understanding applications. Project webpage is available at https://guangyusun.com/f2c ."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02262","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:09:43Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:09:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-19T03:18:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01498","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01498","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01498","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Ou","name":"Ou, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Bi","name":"Bi, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazhen","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Jiazhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiancheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boliang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Boliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Usama","familyName":"Zidan","name":"Zidan, Usama","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Regent","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Regent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vicente","familyName":"Grau","name":"Grau, Vicente","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AortaDiff: A Unified Multitask Diffusion Framework For Contrast-Free AAA Imaging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T22:19:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:12:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-04T19:56:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:04:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While contrast-enhanced CT (CECT) is standard for assessing abdominal aortic aneurysms (AAA), the required iodinated contrast agents pose significant risks, including nephrotoxicity, patient allergies, and environmental harm. To reduce contrast agent use, recent deep learning methods have focused on generating synthetic CECT from non-contrast CT (NCCT) scans. However, most adopt a multi-stage pipeline that first generates images and then performs segmentation, which leads to error accumulation and fails to leverage shared semantic and anatomical structures. To address this, we propose a unified deep learning framework that generates synthetic CECT images from NCCT scans while simultaneously segmenting the aortic lumen and thrombus. Our approach integrates conditional diffusion models (CDM) with multi-task learning, enabling end-to-end joint optimization of image synthesis and anatomical segmentation. Unlike previous multitask diffusion models, our approach requires no initial predictions (e.g., a coarse segmentation mask), shares both encoder and decoder parameters across tasks, and employs a semi-supervised training strategy to learn from scans with missing segmentation labels, a common constraint in real-world clinical data. We evaluated our method on a cohort of 264 patients, where it consistently outperformed state-of-the-art single-task and multi-stage models. For image synthesis, our model achieved a PSNR of 25.61 dB, compared to 23.80 dB from a single-task CDM. For anatomical segmentation, it improved the lumen Dice score to 0.89 from 0.87 and the challenging thrombus Dice score to 0.53 from 0.48 (nnU-Net). These segmentation enhancements led to more accurate clinical measurements, reducing the lumen diameter MAE to 4.19 mm from 5.78 mm and the thrombus area error to 33.85% from 41.45% when compared to nnU-Net. Code is available at https://github.com/yuxuanou623/AortaDiff.git."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"WACV 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01498","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:50:34Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:50:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-08T02:47:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02300","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02300","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02300","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runqian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Runqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Equilibrium Matching: Generative Modeling with Implicit Energy-Based Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:59:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:03:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T15:25:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:03:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T15:39:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T02:04:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Equilibrium Matching (EqM), a generative modeling framework built from an equilibrium dynamics perspective. EqM discards the non-equilibrium, time-conditional dynamics in traditional diffusion and flow-based generative models and instead learns the equilibrium gradient of an implicit energy landscape. Through this approach, we can adopt an optimization-based sampling process at inference time, where samples are obtained by gradient descent on the learned landscape with adjustable step sizes, adaptive optimizers, and adaptive compute. EqM surpasses the generation performance of diffusion/flow models empirically, achieving an FID of 1.90 on ImageNet 256$\\times$256. EqM is also theoretically justified to learn and sample from the data manifold. Beyond generation, EqM is a flexible framework that naturally handles tasks including partially noised image denoising, OOD detection, and image composition. By replacing time-conditional velocities with a unified equilibrium landscape, EqM offers a tighter bridge between flow and energy-based models and a simple route to optimization-driven inference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02300","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:10:39Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:10:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:22:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01795","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01795","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01795","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibo","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Haibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianming","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Lianming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangyu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun Jason","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Chun Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nav-EE: Navigation-Guided Early Exiting for Efficient Vision-Language Models in Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T08:37:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:33:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T08:31:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:33:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) are increasingly applied in autonomous driving for unified perception and reasoning, but high inference latency hinders real-time deployment. Early-exit reduces latency by terminating inference at intermediate layers, yet its task-dependent nature limits generalization across diverse scenarios. We observe that this limitation aligns with autonomous driving: navigation systems can anticipate upcoming contexts (e.g., intersections, traffic lights), indicating which tasks will be required. We propose Nav-EE, a navigation-guided early-exit framework that precomputes task-specific exit layers offline and dynamically applies them online based on navigation priors. Experiments on CODA, Waymo, and BOSCH show that Nav-EE achieves accuracy comparable to full inference while reducing latency by up to 63.9%. Real-vehicle integration with Autoware Universe further demonstrates reduced inference latency (600ms to 300ms), supporting faster decision-making in complex scenarios. These results suggest that coupling navigation foresight with early-exit offers a viable path toward efficient deployment of large models in autonomous systems. Code and data are available at our anonymous repository: https://anonymous.4open.science/r/Nav-EE-BBC4"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01795","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:58:06Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:58:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:16:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01853","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01853","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01853","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladimir","familyName":"Krsmanovic","name":"Krsmanovic, Vladimir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Cosler","name":"Cosler, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Ghanem","name":"Ghanem, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernd","familyName":"Finkbeiner","name":"Finkbeiner, Bernd","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Representations Through Contrastive Neural Model Checking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T09:52:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:37:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T19:40:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:08:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Model checking is a key technique for verifying safety-critical systems against formal specifications, where recent applications of deep learning have shown promise. However, while ubiquitous for vision and language domains, representation learning remains underexplored in formal verification. We introduce Contrastive Neural Model Checking (CNML), a novel method that leverages the model checking task as a guiding signal for learning aligned representations. CNML jointly embeds logical specifications and systems into a shared latent space through a self-supervised contrastive objective. On industry-inspired retrieval tasks, CNML considerably outperforms both algorithmic and neural baselines in cross-modal and intra-modal settings. We further show that the learned representations effectively transfer to downstream tasks and generalize to more complex formulas. These findings demonstrate that model checking can serve as an objective for learning representations for formal languages."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01853","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:59:33Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:59:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:55:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02296","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02296","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02296","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Chien","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Yu-Chien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jr-Jen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jr-Jen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi-Pin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chi-Pin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ci-Siang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Ci-Siang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng-Lin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Meng-Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Chiang Frank","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu-Chiang Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Continual Personalization for Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:58:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:24:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Updating diffusion models in an incremental setting would be practical in real-world applications yet computationally challenging. We present a novel learning strategy of Concept Neuron Selection (CNS), a simple yet effective approach to perform personalization in a continual learning scheme. CNS uniquely identifies neurons in diffusion models that are closely related to the target concepts. In order to mitigate catastrophic forgetting problems while preserving zero-shot text-to-image generation ability, CNS finetunes concept neurons in an incremental manner and jointly preserves knowledge learned of previous concepts. Evaluation of real-world datasets demonstrates that CNS achieves state-of-the-art performance with minimal parameter adjustments, outperforming previous methods in both single and multi-concept personalization works. CNS also achieves fusion-free operation, reducing memory storage and processing time for continual personalization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02296","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:10:33Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:10:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:54:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01456","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01456","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01456","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brett","familyName":"Barkley","name":"Barkley, Brett","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Preston","familyName":"Culbertson","name":"Culbertson, Preston","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Fridovich-Keil","name":"Fridovich-Keil, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SCOPED: Score-Curvature Out-of-distribution Proximity Evaluator for Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T20:54:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:10:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T16:22:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-06T00:49:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection is essential for reliable deployment of machine learning systems in vision, robotics, reinforcement learning, and beyond. We introduce Score-Curvature Out-of-distribution Proximity Evaluator for Diffusion (SCOPED), a fast and general-purpose OOD detection method for diffusion models that reduces the number of forward passes on the trained model by an order of magnitude compared to prior methods, outperforming most diffusion-based baselines and closely approaching the accuracy of the strongest ones. SCOPED is computed from a single diffusion model trained once on a diverse dataset, and combines the Jacobian trace and squared norm of the model's score function into a single test statistic. Rather than thresholding on a fixed value, we estimate the in-distribution density of SCOPED scores using kernel density estimation, enabling a flexible, unsupervised test that, in the simplest case, only requires a single forward pass and one Jacobian-vector product (JVP), made efficient by Hutchinson's trace estimator. On four vision benchmarks, SCOPED achieves competitive or state-of-the-art precision-recall scores despite its low computational cost. The same method generalizes to robotic control tasks with shared state and action spaces, identifying distribution shifts across reward functions and training regimes. These results position SCOPED as a practical foundation for fast and reliable OOD detection in real-world domains, including perceptual artifacts in vision, outlier detection in autoregressive models, exploration in reinforcement learning, and dataset curation for unsupervised training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01456","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:49:33Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:49:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-06T02:04:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01494","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01494","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01494","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isha","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Isha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rylan","familyName":"Schaeffer","name":"Schaeffer, Rylan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua","familyName":"Kazdan","name":"Kazdan, Joshua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ken Ziyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ken Ziyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanmi","familyName":"Koyejo","name":"Koyejo, Sanmi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding Adversarial Transfer: Why Representation-Space Attacks Fail Where Data-Space Attacks Succeed"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T22:10:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:12:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T11:35:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-06T00:35:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The field of adversarial robustness has long established that adversarial examples can successfully transfer between image classifiers and that text jailbreaks can successfully transfer between language models (LMs). However, a pair of recent studies reported being unable to successfully transfer image jailbreaks between vision-language models (VLMs). To explain this striking difference, we propose a fundamental distinction regarding the transferability of attacks against machine learning models: attacks in the input data-space can transfer, whereas attacks in model representation space do not, at least not without geometric alignment of representations. We then provide theoretical and empirical evidence of this hypothesis in four different settings. First, we mathematically prove this distinction in a simple setting where two networks compute the same input-output map but via different representations. Second, we construct representation-space attacks against image classifiers that are as successful as well-known data-space attacks, but fail to transfer. Third, we construct representation-space attacks against LMs that successfully jailbreak the attacked models but again fail to transfer. Fourth, we construct data-space attacks against VLMs that successfully transfer to new VLMs, and we show that representation space attacks can transfer when VLMs' latent geometries are sufficiently aligned in post-projector space. Our work reveals that adversarial transfer is not an inherent property of all attacks but contingent on their operational domain - the shared data-space versus models' unique representation spaces - a critical insight for building more robust models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01494","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:50:29Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:50:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-06T02:03:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01669","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01669","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01669","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongrui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hongrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Ziyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hujun","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Hujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sida","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Sida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UniVerse: Unleashing the Scene Prior of Video Diffusion Models for Robust Radiance Field Reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T04:50:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:25:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T04:06:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-06T00:20:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper tackles the challenge of robust reconstruction, i.e., the task of reconstructing a 3D scene from a set of inconsistent multi-view images. Some recent works have attempted to simultaneously remove image inconsistencies and perform reconstruction by integrating image degradation modeling into neural 3D scene representations. However, these methods rely heavily on dense observations for robustly optimizing model parameters. To address this issue, we propose to decouple robust reconstruction into two subtasks: restoration and reconstruction, which naturally simplifies the optimization process. To this end, we introduce UniVerse, a unified framework for robust reconstruction based on a video diffusion model. Specifically, UniVerse first converts inconsistent images into initial videos, then uses a specially designed video diffusion model to restore them into consistent images, and finally reconstructs the 3D scenes from these restored images. Compared with case-by-case per-view degradation modeling, the diffusion model learns a general scene prior from large-scale data, making it applicable to diverse image inconsistencies. Extensive experiments on both synthetic and real-world datasets demonstrate the strong generalization capability and superior performance of our method in robust reconstruction. Moreover, UniVerse can control the style of the reconstructed 3D scene. Project page: https://jin-cao-tma.github.io/UniVerse.github.io/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"page: https://jin-cao-tma.github.io/UniVerse.github.io/ code: https://github.com/zju3dv/UniVerse"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01669","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:54:56Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:54:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-06T02:01:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02305","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02305","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02305","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyler","familyName":"Farghly","name":"Farghly, Tyler","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Potaptchik","name":"Potaptchik, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Howard","name":"Howard, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Deligiannidis","name":"Deligiannidis, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakiw","familyName":"Pidstrigach","name":"Pidstrigach, Jakiw","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis: Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:59:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:04:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have achieved state-of-the-art performance, demonstrating remarkable generalisation capabilities across diverse domains. However, the mechanisms underpinning these strong capabilities remain only partially understood. A leading conjecture, based on the manifold hypothesis, attributes this success to their ability to adapt to low-dimensional geometric structure within the data. This work provides evidence for this conjecture, focusing on how such phenomena could result from the formulation of the learning problem through score matching. We inspect the role of implicit regularisation by investigating the effect of smoothing minimisers of the empirical score matching objective. Our theoretical and empirical results confirm that smoothing the score function -- or equivalently, smoothing in the log-density domain -- produces smoothing tangential to the data manifold. In addition, we show that the manifold along which the diffusion model generalises can be controlled by choosing an appropriate smoothing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02305","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:10:46Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:10:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:10:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02292","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02292","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02292","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hala","familyName":"Sheta","name":"Sheta, Hala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuyu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shuyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilia","familyName":"Alenabi","name":"Alenabi, Ilia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryker","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Ryker","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxi","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Ruoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqiao","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ziqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Freda","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Freda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Behavioral Performance to Internal Competence: Interpreting Vision-Language Models with VLM-Lens"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:58:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:03:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce VLM-Lens, a toolkit designed to enable systematic benchmarking, analysis, and interpretation of vision-language models (VLMs) by supporting the extraction of intermediate outputs from any layer during the forward pass of open-source VLMs. VLM-Lens provides a unified, YAML-configurable interface that abstracts away model-specific complexities and supports user-friendly operation across diverse VLMs. It currently supports 16 state-of-the-art base VLMs and their over 30 variants, and is extensible to accommodate new models without changing the core logic.\n The toolkit integrates easily with various interpretability and analysis methods. We demonstrate its usage with two simple analytical experiments, revealing systematic differences in the hidden representations of VLMs across layers and target concepts. VLM-Lens is released as an open-sourced project to accelerate community efforts in understanding and improving VLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 System Demonstration | Code: https://github.com/compling-wat/vlm-lens"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02292","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:10:27Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:10:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:10:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02287","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02287","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02287","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Torralba","name":"Torralba, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MultiModal Action Conditioned Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:57:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:03:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current video models fail as world model as they lack fine-graiend control. General-purpose household robots require real-time fine motor control to handle delicate tasks and urgent situations. In this work, we introduce fine-grained multimodal actions to capture such precise control. We consider senses of proprioception, kinesthesia, force haptics, and muscle activation. Such multimodal senses naturally enables fine-grained interactions that are difficult to simulate with text-conditioned generative models. To effectively simulate fine-grained multisensory actions, we develop a feature learning paradigm that aligns these modalities while preserving the unique information each modality provides. We further propose a regularization scheme to enhance causality of the action trajectory features in representing intricate interaction dynamics. Experiments show that incorporating multimodal senses improves simulation accuracy and reduces temporal drift. Extensive ablation studies and downstream applications demonstrate the effectiveness and practicality of our work."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02287","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:10:20Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:10:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:10:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02283","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02283","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02283","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justin","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Justin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanhao","familyName":"Ban","name":"Ban, Yuanhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cho-Jui","familyName":"Hsieh","name":"Hsieh, Cho-Jui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Forcing++: Towards Minute-Scale High-Quality Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:55:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:03:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have revolutionized image and video generation, achieving unprecedented visual quality. However, their reliance on transformer architectures incurs prohibitively high computational costs, particularly when extending generation to long videos. Recent work has explored autoregressive formulations for long video generation, typically by distilling from short-horizon bidirectional teachers. Nevertheless, given that teacher models cannot synthesize long videos, the extrapolation of student models beyond their training horizon often leads to pronounced quality degradation, arising from the compounding of errors within the continuous latent space. In this paper, we propose a simple yet effective approach to mitigate quality degradation in long-horizon video generation without requiring supervision from long-video teachers or retraining on long video datasets. Our approach centers on exploiting the rich knowledge of teacher models to provide guidance for the student model through sampled segments drawn from self-generated long videos. Our method maintains temporal consistency while scaling video length by up to 20x beyond teacher's capability, avoiding common issues such as over-exposure and error-accumulation without recomputing overlapping frames like previous methods. When scaling up the computation, our method shows the capability of generating videos up to 4 minutes and 15 seconds, equivalent to 99.9% of the maximum span supported by our base model's position embedding and more than 50x longer than that of our baseline model. Experiments on standard benchmarks and our proposed improved benchmark demonstrate that our approach substantially outperforms baseline methods in both fidelity and consistency. Our long-horizon videos demo can be found at https://self-forcing-plus-plus.github.io/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02283","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:10:14Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:10:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:10:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02270","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02270","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02270","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sathira","familyName":"Silva","name":"Silva, Sathira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eman","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Eman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chetan","familyName":"Arora","name":"Arora, Chetan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Haris","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Muhammad Haris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"microCLIP: Unsupervised CLIP Adaptation via Coarse-Fine Token Fusion for Fine-Grained Image Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:47:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T01:02:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unsupervised adaptation of CLIP-based vision-language models (VLMs) for fine-grained image classification requires sensitivity to microscopic local cues. While CLIP exhibits strong zero-shot transfer, its reliance on coarse global features restricts its performance on fine-grained classification tasks. Prior efforts inject fine-grained knowledge by aligning large language model (LLM) descriptions with the CLIP $\\texttt{[CLS]}$ token; however, this approach overlooks spatial precision. We propose $\\textbf{microCLIP}$, a self-training framework that jointly refines CLIP's visual and textual representations using fine-grained cues. At its core is Saliency-Oriented Attention Pooling (SOAP) within a lightweight TokenFusion module, which builds a saliency-guided $\\texttt{[FG]}$ token from patch embeddings and fuses it with the global $\\texttt{[CLS]}$ token for coarse-fine alignment. To stabilize adaptation, we introduce a two-headed LLM-derived classifier: a frozen classifier that, via multi-view alignment, provides a stable text-based prior for pseudo-labeling, and a learnable classifier initialized from LLM descriptions and fine-tuned with TokenFusion. We further develop Dynamic Knowledge Aggregation, which convexly combines fixed LLM/CLIP priors with TokenFusion's evolving logits to iteratively refine pseudo-labels. Together, these components uncover latent fine-grained signals in CLIP, yielding a consistent $2.90\\%$ average accuracy gain across 13 fine-grained benchmarks while requiring only light adaptation. Our code is available at https://github.com/sathiiii/microCLIP."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02270","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:09:55Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:09:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:09:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02216","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02216","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02216","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeqi","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Zeqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minshuo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Minshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusion Transformers for Imputation: Statistical Efficiency and Uncertainty Quantification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:00:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:59:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Imputation methods play a critical role in enhancing the quality of practical time-series data, which often suffer from pervasive missing values. Recently, diffusion-based generative imputation methods have demonstrated remarkable success compared to autoregressive and conventional statistical approaches. Despite their empirical success, the theoretical understanding of how well diffusion-based models capture complex spatial and temporal dependencies between the missing values and observed ones remains limited. Our work addresses this gap by investigating the statistical efficiency of conditional diffusion transformers for imputation and quantifying the uncertainty in missing values. Specifically, we derive statistical sample complexity bounds based on a novel approximation theory for conditional score functions using transformers, and, through this, construct tight confidence regions for missing values. Our findings also reveal that the efficiency and accuracy of imputation are significantly influenced by the missing patterns. Furthermore, we validate these theoretical insights through simulation and propose a mixed-masking training strategy to enhance the imputation performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"49 pages, 4 figures. Accepted as a poster at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02216","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:08:34Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:08:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:08:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02204","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02204","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02204","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingzhong","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Lingzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaibo","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shuaibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyue","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Haiyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengzhou","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Pengzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongru","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zongru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gongshen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Gongshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuosheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhuosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Say One Thing, Do Another? Diagnosing Reasoning-Execution Gaps in VLM-Powered Mobile-Use Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:51:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:59:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mobile-use agents powered by vision-language models (VLMs) have shown great potential in interpreting natural language instructions and generating corresponding actions based on mobile graphical user interface. Recent studies suggest that incorporating chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning tends to improve the execution accuracy. However, existing evaluations emphasize execution accuracy while neglecting whether CoT reasoning aligns with ground-truth actions. This oversight fails to assess potential reasoning-execution gaps, which in turn foster over-trust: users relying on seemingly plausible CoTs may unknowingly authorize harmful actions, potentially resulting in financial loss or trust crisis. In this work, we introduce a new evaluation framework to diagnose reasoning-execution gaps. At its core lies Ground-Truth Alignment (GTA), which measures whether the action implied by a CoT matches the ground-truth action. By combining GTA with the standard Exact Match (EM) metric, we jointly assess both the reasoning accuracy and execution accuracy. This joint perspective reveals two types of reasoning-execution gaps: (i) Execution Gap (EG), where the reasoning correctly identifies the correct action but execution fails, and (ii) Reasoning Gap (RG), where execution succeeds but reasoning process conflicts with the actual execution. Experimental results across a wide range of mobile interaction tasks reveal that reasoning-execution gaps are prevalent, with execution gaps occurring more frequently than reasoning gaps. Moreover, while scaling up model size reduces the overall gap, sizable execution gaps persist even in the largest models. Further analysis shows that our framework reliably reflects systematic EG/RG patterns in state-of-the-art models. These findings offer concrete diagnostics and support the development of more trustworthy mobile-use agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02204","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:08:17Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:08:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:08:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02155","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02155","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02155","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Wesemann","name":"Wesemann, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Waschkowski","name":"Waschkowski, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhaoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unlocking Vision-Language Models for Video Anomaly Detection via Fine-Grained Prompting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:06:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:56:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prompting has emerged as a practical way to adapt frozen vision-language models (VLMs) for video anomaly detection (VAD). Yet, existing prompts are often overly abstract, overlooking the fine-grained human-object interactions or action semantics that define complex anomalies in surveillance videos. We propose ASK-Hint, a structured prompting framework that leverages action-centric knowledge to elicit more accurate and interpretable reasoning from frozen VLMs. Our approach organizes prompts into semantically coherent groups (e.g. violence, property crimes, public safety) and formulates fine-grained guiding questions that align model predictions with discriminative visual cues. Extensive experiments on UCF-Crime and XD-Violence show that ASK-Hint consistently improves AUC over prior baselines, achieving state-of-the-art performance compared to both fine-tuned and training-free methods. Beyond accuracy, our framework provides interpretable reasoning traces towards anomaly and demonstrates strong generalization across datasets and VLM backbones. These results highlight the critical role of prompt granularity and establish ASK-Hint as a new training-free and generalizable solution for explainable video anomaly detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, video anomaly detection"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02155","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:07:03Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:07:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:07:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02148","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02148","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02148","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianing","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Jianing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhigang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhigang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Policy Gradient Guidance Enables Test Time Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:00:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:55:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Policy Gradient Guidance (PGG), a simple extension of classifier-free guidance from diffusion models to classical policy gradient methods. PGG augments the policy gradient with an unconditional branch and interpolates conditional and unconditional branches, yielding a test-time control knob that modulates behavior without retraining. We provide a theoretical derivation showing that the additional normalization term vanishes under advantage estimation, leading to a clean guided policy gradient update. Empirically, we evaluate PGG on discrete and continuous control benchmarks. We find that conditioning dropout-central to diffusion guidance-offers gains in simple discrete tasks and low sample regimes, but dropout destabilizes continuous control. Training with modestly larger guidance ($γ\u0026gt;1$) consistently improves stability, sample efficiency, and controllability. Our results show that guidance, previously confined to diffusion policies, can be adapted to standard on-policy methods, opening new directions for controllable online reinforcement learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02148","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:06:53Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:06:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:06:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02114","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02114","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02114","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding-Ruei","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Ding-Ruei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FRIEREN: Federated Learning with Vision-Language Regularization for Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T15:21:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:53:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federeated Learning (FL) offers a privacy-preserving solution for Semantic Segmentation (SS) tasks to adapt to new domains, but faces significant challenges from these domain shifts, particularly when client data is unlabeled. However, most existing FL methods unrealistically assume access to labeled data on remote clients or fail to leverage the power of modern Vision Foundation Models (VFMs). Here, we propose a novel and challenging task, FFREEDG, in which a model is pretrained on a server's labeled source dataset and subsequently trained across clients using only their unlabeled data, without ever re-accessing the source. To solve FFREEDG, we propose FRIEREN, a framework that leverages the knowledge of a VFM by integrating vision and language modalities. Our approach employs a Vision-Language decoder guided by CLIP-based text embeddings to improve semantic disambiguation and uses a weak-to-strong consistency learning strategy for robust local training on pseudo-labels. Our experiments on synthetic-to-real and clear-to-adverse-weather benchmarks demonstrate that our framework effectively tackles this new task, achieving competitive performance against established domain generalization and adaptation methods and setting a strong baseline for future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Master Thesis"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02114","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:06:04Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:06:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:06:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02110","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02110","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02110","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koichi","familyName":"Saito","name":"Saito, Koichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Tanke","name":"Tanke, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Simon","name":"Simon, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masato","familyName":"Ishii","name":"Ishii, Masato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuki","familyName":"Shimada","name":"Shimada, Kazuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zachary","familyName":"Novack","name":"Novack, Zachary","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akio","familyName":"Hayakawa","name":"Hayakawa, Akio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Shibuya","name":"Shibuya, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Mitsufuji","name":"Mitsufuji, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SoundReactor: Frame-level Online Video-to-Audio Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T15:18:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:53:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prevailing Video-to-Audio (V2A) generation models operate offline, assuming an entire video sequence or chunks of frames are available beforehand. This critically limits their use in interactive applications such as live content creation and emerging generative world models. To address this gap, we introduce the novel task of frame-level online V2A generation, where a model autoregressively generates audio from video without access to future video frames. Furthermore, we propose SoundReactor, which, to the best of our knowledge, is the first simple yet effective framework explicitly tailored for this task. Our design enforces end-to-end causality and targets low per-frame latency with audio-visual synchronization. Our model's backbone is a decoder-only causal transformer over continuous audio latents. For vision conditioning, it leverages grid (patch) features extracted from the smallest variant of the DINOv2 vision encoder, which are aggregated into a single token per frame to maintain end-to-end causality and efficiency. The model is trained through a diffusion pre-training followed by consistency fine-tuning to accelerate the diffusion head decoding. On a benchmark of diverse gameplay videos from AAA titles, our model successfully generates semantically and temporally aligned, high-quality full-band stereo audio, validated by both objective and human evaluations. Furthermore, our model achieves low per-frame waveform-level latency (26.3ms with the head NFE=1, 31.5ms with NFE=4) on 30FPS, 480p videos using a single H100. Demo samples are available at https://koichi-saito-sony.github.io/soundreactor/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02110","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:05:58Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:05:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:05:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02086","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02086","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02086","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Behnam","name":"Behnam, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VGDM: Vision-Guided Diffusion Model for Brain Tumor Detection and Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T14:52:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:51:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate detection and segmentation of brain tumors from magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) are essential for diagnosis, treatment planning, and clinical monitoring. While convolutional architectures such as U-Net have long been the backbone of medical image segmentation, their limited capacity to capture long-range dependencies constrains performance on complex tumor structures. Recent advances in diffusion models have demonstrated strong potential for generating high-fidelity medical images and refining segmentation boundaries.\n In this work, we propose VGDM: Vision-Guided Diffusion Model for Brain Tumor Detection and Segmentation framework, a transformer-driven diffusion framework for brain tumor detection and segmentation. By embedding a vision transformer at the core of the diffusion process, the model leverages global contextual reasoning together with iterative denoising to enhance both volumetric accuracy and boundary precision. The transformer backbone enables more effective modeling of spatial relationships across entire MRI volumes, while diffusion refinement mitigates voxel-level errors and recovers fine-grained tumor details.\n This hybrid design provides a pathway toward improved robustness and scalability in neuro-oncology, moving beyond conventional U-Net baselines. Experimental validation on MRI brain tumor datasets demonstrates consistent gains in Dice similarity and Hausdorff distance, underscoring the potential of transformer-guided diffusion models to advance the state of the art in tumor segmentation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02086","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:05:21Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:05:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:05:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01967","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01967","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01967","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aadarsh Anantha","familyName":"Ramakrishnan","name":"Ramakrishnan, Aadarsh Anantha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubham","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Shubham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Selvanayagam","familyName":"S","name":"S, Selvanayagam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunwar","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Kunwar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ZK-WAGON: Imperceptible Watermark for Image Generation Models using ZK-SNARKs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T12:39:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:43:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As image generation models grow increasingly powerful and accessible, concerns around authenticity, ownership, and misuse of synthetic media have become critical. The ability to generate lifelike images indistinguishable from real ones introduces risks such as misinformation, deepfakes, and intellectual property violations. Traditional watermarking methods either degrade image quality, are easily removed, or require access to confidential model internals - making them unsuitable for secure and scalable deployment. We are the first to introduce ZK-WAGON, a novel system for watermarking image generation models using the Zero-Knowledge Succinct Non Interactive Argument of Knowledge (ZK-SNARKs). Our approach enables verifiable proof of origin without exposing model weights, generation prompts, or any sensitive internal information. We propose Selective Layer ZK-Circuit Creation (SL-ZKCC), a method to selectively convert key layers of an image generation model into a circuit, reducing proof generation time significantly. Generated ZK-SNARK proofs are imperceptibly embedded into a generated image via Least Significant Bit (LSB) steganography. We demonstrate this system on both GAN and Diffusion models, providing a secure, model-agnostic pipeline for trustworthy AI image generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at AI-ML Systems 2025, Bangalore, India, https://www.aimlsystems.org/2025/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01967","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:02:23Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:02:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:02:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01954","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01954","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01954","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongyi","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haojie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanqing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Nanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyi","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Jingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyi","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Junyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofen","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Xiaofen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nancy F.","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Nancy F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuicheng","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Shuicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xulei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xulei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Patch-as-Decodable-Token: Towards Unified Multi-Modal Vision Tasks in MLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T12:23:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:42:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have advanced rapidly in recent years. However, existing approaches for vision tasks often rely on indirect representations, such as generating coordinates as text for detection, which limits performance and prevents dense prediction tasks like segmentation. To overcome these challenges, we introduce Patch-as-Decodable Token (PaDT), a unified paradigm that enables MLLMs to directly generate both textual and diverse visual outputs. Central to PaDT are Visual Reference Tokens (VRTs), derived from visual patch embeddings of query images and interleaved seamlessly with LLM's output textual tokens. A lightweight decoder then transforms LLM's outputs into detection, segmentation, and grounding predictions. Unlike prior methods, PaDT processes VRTs independently at each forward pass and dynamically expands the embedding table, thus improving localization and differentiation among similar objects. We further tailor a training strategy for PaDT by randomly selecting VRTs for supervised fine-tuning and introducing a robust per-token cross-entropy loss. Our empirical studies across four visual perception and understanding tasks suggest PaDT consistently achieving state-of-the-art performance, even compared with significantly larger MLLM models. The code is available at https://github.com/Gorilla-Lab-SCUT/PaDT."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 12 figures and 9 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01954","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T02:02:04Z","registered":"2025-10-03T02:02:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T02:02:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01845","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01845","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01845","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ece","familyName":"Takmaz","name":"Takmaz, Ece","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lisa","familyName":"Bylinina","name":"Bylinina, Lisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Dotlacil","name":"Dotlacil, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Model Merging to Maintain Language-Only Performance in Developmentally Plausible Multimodal Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T09:38:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:36:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"State-of-the-art vision-and-language models consist of many parameters and learn from enormous datasets, surpassing the amounts of linguistic data that children are exposed to as they acquire a language. This paper presents our approach to the multimodal track of the BabyLM challenge addressing this discrepancy. We develop language-only and multimodal models in low-resource settings using developmentally plausible datasets, with our multimodal models outperforming previous BabyLM baselines. One finding in the multimodal language model literature is that these models tend to underperform in \\textit{language-only} tasks. Therefore, we focus on maintaining language-only abilities in multimodal models. To this end, we experiment with \\textit{model merging}, where we fuse the parameters of multimodal models with those of language-only models using weighted linear interpolation. Our results corroborate the findings that multimodal models underperform in language-only benchmarks that focus on grammar, and model merging with text-only models can help alleviate this problem to some extent, while maintaining multimodal performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the EMNLP 2025 workshop BabyLM: Accelerating language modeling research with cognitively plausible datasets"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01845","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:59:21Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:59:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:59:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01749","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01749","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01749","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"Delchevalerie","name":"Delchevalerie, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnaud","familyName":"Bougaham","name":"Bougaham, Arnaud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Mayer","name":"Mayer, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoît","familyName":"Frénay","name":"Frénay, Benoît","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michaël","familyName":"Lobet","name":"Lobet, Michaël","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Photonic Band Diagram Generation with Transformer-Latent Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T07:35:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:30:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Photonic crystals enable fine control over light propagation at the nanoscale, and thus play a central role in the development of photonic and quantum technologies. Photonic band diagrams (BDs) are a key tool to investigate light propagation into such inhomogeneous structured materials. However, computing BDs requires solving Maxwell's equations across many configurations, making it numerically expensive, especially when embedded in optimization loops for inverse design techniques, for example. To address this challenge, we introduce the first approach for BD generation based on diffusion models, with the capacity to later generalize and scale to arbitrary three dimensional structures. Our method couples a transformer encoder, which extracts contextual embeddings from the input structure, with a latent diffusion model to generate the corresponding BD. In addition, we provide insights into why transformers and diffusion models are well suited to capture the complex interference and scattering phenomena inherent to photonics, paving the way for new surrogate modeling strategies in this domain."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01749","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:56:58Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:56:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:56:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01700","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01700","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01700","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rohan","familyName":"Wadhawan","name":"Wadhawan, Rohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrice Y","familyName":"Harel-Canada","name":"Harel-Canada, Fabrice Y","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zi-Yi","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Zi-Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhaila","familyName":"Shakiah","name":"Shakiah, Suhaila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robinson","familyName":"Piramuthu","name":"Piramuthu, Robinson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Nanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VaPR -- Vision-language Preference alignment for Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T06:10:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:27:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Preference finetuning methods like Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) with AI-generated feedback have shown promise in aligning Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) with human preferences. However, existing techniques overlook the prevalence of noise in synthetic preference annotations in the form of stylistic and length biases. To this end, we introduce a hard-negative response generation framework based on LLM-guided response editing, that produces rejected responses with targeted errors, maintaining stylistic and length similarity to the accepted ones. Using this framework, we develop the VaPR dataset, comprising 30K high-quality samples, to finetune three LVLM families: LLaVA-V1.5, Qwen2VL \u0026amp; Qwen2.5VL (2B-13B sizes). Our VaPR models deliver significant performance improvements across ten benchmarks, achieving average gains of 6.5% (LLaVA), 4.0% (Qwen2VL), and 1.5% (Qwen2.5VL), with notable improvements on reasoning tasks. A scaling analysis shows that performance consistently improves with data size, with LLaVA models benefiting even at smaller scales. Moreover, VaPR reduces the tendency to answer \"Yes\" in binary questions - addressing a common failure mode in LVLMs like LLaVA. Lastly, we show that the framework generalizes to open-source LLMs as editors, with models trained on VaPR-OS achieving ~99% of the performance of models trained on \\name, which is synthesized using GPT-4o. Our data, models, and code can be found on the project page https://vap-r.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01700","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:55:42Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:55:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:55:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuchen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuzhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renjie","familyName":"Pi","name":"Pi, Renjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Look Less, Reason More: Rollout-Guided Adaptive Pixel-Space Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T05:14:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:25:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) excel at many multimodal tasks, yet they frequently struggle with tasks requiring precise understanding and handling of fine-grained visual elements. This is mainly due to information loss during image encoding or insufficient attention to critical regions. Recent work has shown promise by incorporating pixel-level visual information into the reasoning process, enabling VLMs to access high-resolution visual details during their thought process. However, this pixel-level information is often overused, leading to inefficiency and distraction from irrelevant visual details. To address these challenges, we propose the first framework for adaptive pixel reasoning that dynamically determines necessary pixel-level operations based on the input query. Specifically, we first apply operation-aware supervised fine-tuning to establish baseline competence in textual reasoning and visual operations, then design a novel rollout-guided reinforcement learning framework relying on feedback of the model's own responses, which enables the VLM to determine when pixel operations should be invoked based on query difficulty. Experiments on extensive multimodal reasoning benchmarks show that our model achieves superior performance while significantly reducing unnecessary visual operations. Impressively, our model achieves 73.4\\% accuracy on HR-Bench 4K while maintaining a tool usage ratio of only 20.1\\%, improving accuracy and simultaneously reducing tool usage by 66.5\\% compared to the previous methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint, Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:55:14Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:55:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:55:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01649","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01649","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01649","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Tanzil","familyName":"Furqon","name":"Furqon, Muhammad Tanzil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahardhika","familyName":"Pratama","name":"Pratama, Mahardhika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor","familyName":"Škrjanc","name":"Škrjanc, Igor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Habibullah","familyName":"Habibullah","name":"Habibullah, Habibullah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kutluyil","familyName":"Dogancay","name":"Dogancay, Kutluyil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Source-Free Cross-Domain Continual Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T04:09:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:24:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although existing cross-domain continual learning approaches successfully address many streaming tasks having domain shifts, they call for a fully labeled source domain hindering their feasibility in the privacy constrained environments. This paper goes one step ahead with the problem of source-free cross-domain continual learning where the use of source-domain samples are completely prohibited. We propose the idea of rehearsal-free frequency-aware dynamic prompt collaborations (REFEREE) to cope with the absence of labeled source-domain samples in realm of cross-domain continual learning. REFEREE is built upon a synergy between a source-pre-trained model and a large-scale vision-language model, thus overcoming the problem of sub-optimal generalizations when relying only on a source pre-trained model. The domain shift problem between the source domain and the target domain is handled by a frequency-aware prompting technique encouraging low-frequency components while suppressing high-frequency components. This strategy generates frequency-aware augmented samples, robust against noisy pseudo labels. The noisy pseudo-label problem is further addressed with the uncertainty-aware weighting strategy where the mean and covariance matrix are weighted by prediction uncertainties, thus mitigating the adverse effects of the noisy pseudo label. Besides, the issue of catastrophic forgetting (CF) is overcome by kernel linear discriminant analysis (KLDA) where the backbone network is frozen while the classification is performed using the linear discriminant analysis approach guided by the random kernel method. Our rigorous numerical studies confirm the advantage of our approach where it beats prior arts having access to source domain samples with significant margins."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01649","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:54:22Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:54:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:54:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01623","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01623","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01623","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angen","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Angen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dapeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dapeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VLA-R1: Enhancing Reasoning in Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:54:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:21:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models aim to unify perception, language understanding, and action generation, offering strong cross-task and cross-scene generalization with broad impact on embodied AI. However, current VLA models often lack explicit step-by-step reasoning, instead emitting final actions without considering affordance constraints or geometric relations. Their post-training pipelines also rarely reinforce reasoning quality, relying primarily on supervised fine-tuning with weak reward design. To address these challenges, we present VLA-R1, a reasoning-enhanced VLA that integrates Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to systematically optimize both reasoning and execution. Specifically, we design an RLVR-based post-training strategy with verifiable rewards for region alignment, trajectory consistency, and output formatting, thereby strengthening reasoning robustness and execution accuracy. Moreover, we develop VLA-CoT-13K, a high-quality dataset that provides chain-of-thought supervision explicitly aligned with affordance and trajectory annotations. Furthermore, extensive evaluations on in-domain, out-of-domain, simulation, and real-robot platforms demonstrate that VLA-R1 achieves superior generalization and real-world performance compared to prior VLA methods. We plan to release the model, code, and dataset following the publication of this work. Code: https://github.com/GigaAI-research/VLA-R1. Website: https://gigaai-research.github.io/VLA-R1."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01623","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:53:44Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:53:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:53:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01582","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01582","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01582","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krishna Teja","familyName":"Chitty-Venkata","name":"Chitty-Venkata, Krishna Teja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murali","familyName":"Emani","name":"Emani, Murali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ImageNet-Think-250K: A Large-Scale Synthetic Dataset for Multimodal Reasoning for Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:02:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:18:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop ImageNet-Think, a multimodal reasoning dataset designed to aid the development of Vision Language Models (VLMs) with explicit reasoning capabilities. Our dataset is built on 250,000 images from ImageNet21k dataset, providing structured thinking tokens and corresponding answers. Our synthetic dataset is generated by two state-of-the-art VLMs: GLM-4.1V-9B-Thinking and Kimi-VL-A3B-Thinking-2506. Each image is accompanied by two pairs of thinking-answer sequences, creating a resource for training and evaluating multimodal reasoning models. We capture the step-by-step reasoning process of VLMs and the final descriptive answers. Our goal with this dataset is to enable the development of more robust VLMs while contributing to the broader understanding of multimodal reasoning mechanisms. The dataset and evaluation benchmarks will be publicly available to aid research in reasoning/thinking multimodal VLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01582","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:52:43Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:52:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:52:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01576","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01576","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01576","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricardo Gonzalez","familyName":"Penuela","name":"Penuela, Ricardo Gonzalez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felipe","familyName":"Arias-Russi","name":"Arias-Russi, Felipe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Capriles","name":"Capriles, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Guiding Multimodal Large Language Models with Blind and Low Vision People Visual Questions for Proactive Visual Interpretations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.m; H.5.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T01:48:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:18:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have been integrated into visual interpretation applications to support Blind and Low Vision (BLV) users because of their accuracy and ability to provide rich, human-like interpretations. However, these applications often default to comprehensive, lengthy descriptions regardless of context. This leads to inefficient exchanges, as users must go through irrelevant details rather than receiving the specific information they are likely to seek. To deliver more contextually-relevant information, we developed a system that draws on historical BLV users questions. When given an image, our system identifies similar past visual contexts from the VizWiz-LF dataset and uses the associated questions to guide the MLLM generate descriptions more relevant to BLV users. An evaluation with three human labelers who revised 92 context-aware and context-free descriptions showed that context-aware descriptions anticipated and answered users' questions in 76.1% of cases (70 out of 92) and were preferred in 54.4% of comparisons (50 out of 92). Our paper reviews, and data analysis are publicly available in a Github repository at https://github.com/rgonzalezp/guiding-multimodal-large-language-models-with-blind-and-low-vision-people-visual-questions ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 2 figure, 2 tables, CV4A11y Workshop at ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01576","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:52:34Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:52:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:52:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01549","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01549","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01549","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Utsav","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Utsav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirudh","familyName":"Thatipelli","name":"Thatipelli, Anirudh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Souradip","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Souradip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anit Kumar","familyName":"Sahu","name":"Sahu, Anit Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Furong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Furong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amrit Singh","familyName":"Bedi","name":"Bedi, Amrit Singh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mubarak","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Mubarak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MIRA: Towards Mitigating Reward Hacking in Inference-Time Alignment of T2I Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T00:47:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:16:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models excel at generating images conditioned on text prompts, but the resulting images often do not satisfy user-specific criteria measured by scalar rewards such as Aesthetic Scores. This alignment typically requires fine-tuning, which is computationally demanding. Recently, inference-time alignment via noise optimization has emerged as an efficient alternative, modifying initial input noise to steer the diffusion denoising process towards generating high-reward images. However, this approach suffers from reward hacking, where the model produces images that score highly, yet deviate significantly from the original prompt. We show that noise-space regularization is insufficient and that preventing reward hacking requires an explicit image-space constraint. To this end, we propose MIRA (MItigating Reward hAcking), a training-free, inference-time alignment method. MIRA introduces an image-space, score-based KL surrogate that regularizes the sampling trajectory with a frozen backbone, constraining the output distribution so reward can increase without off-distribution drift (reward hacking). We derive a tractable approximation to KL using diffusion scores. Across SDv1.5 and SDXL, multiple rewards (Aesthetic, HPSv2, PickScore), and public datasets (e.g., Animal-Animal, HPDv2), MIRA achieves \u0026gt;60\\% win rate vs. strong baselines while preserving prompt adherence; mechanism plots show reward gains with near-zero drift, whereas DNO drifts as compute increases. We further introduce MIRA-DPO, mapping preference optimization to inference time with a frozen backbone, extending MIRA to non-differentiable rewards without fine-tuning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01549","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:51:54Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:51:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:51:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01546","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01546","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01546","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanchuan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Shanchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Shrivastava","name":"Shrivastava, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhenheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Growing Visual Generative Capacity for Pre-Trained MLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T00:40:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:16:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) extend the success of language models to visual understanding, and recent efforts have sought to build unified MLLMs that support both understanding and generation. However, constructing such models remains challenging: hybrid approaches combine continuous embeddings with diffusion or flow-based objectives, producing high-quality images but breaking the autoregressive paradigm, while pure autoregressive approaches unify text and image prediction over discrete visual tokens but often face trade-offs between semantic alignment and pixel-level fidelity. In this work, we present Bridge, a pure autoregressive unified MLLM that augments pre-trained visual understanding models with generative ability through a Mixture-of-Transformers architecture, enabling both image understanding and generation within a single next-token prediction framework. To further improve visual generation fidelity, we propose a semantic-to-pixel discrete representation that integrates compact semantic tokens with fine-grained pixel tokens, achieving strong language alignment and precise description of visual details with only a 7.9% increase in sequence length. Extensive experiments across diverse multimodal benchmarks demonstrate that Bridge achieves competitive or superior results in both understanding and generation benchmarks, while requiring less training data and reduced training time compared to prior unified MLLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://hywang66.github.io/bridge/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01546","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:51:50Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:51:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:51:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01540","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01540","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01540","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiamu","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Jiamu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meilong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Meilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weitao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Weitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kiwan","familyName":"Maeng","name":"Maeng, Kiwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Kifer","name":"Kifer, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Better Optimization For Listwise Preference in Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T00:26:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:15:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) has proven effectiveness for aligning text-to-image (T2I) diffusion models with human preferences. Although Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) is widely adopted for its computational efficiency and avoidance of explicit reward modeling, its applications to diffusion models have primarily relied on pairwise preferences. The precise optimization of listwise preferences remains largely unaddressed. In practice, human feedback on image preferences often contains implicit ranked information, which conveys more precise human preferences than pairwise comparisons. In this work, we propose Diffusion-LPO, a simple and effective framework for Listwise Preference Optimization in diffusion models with listwise data. Given a caption, we aggregate user feedback into a ranked list of images and derive a listwise extension of the DPO objective under the Plackett-Luce model. Diffusion-LPO enforces consistency across the entire ranking by encouraging each sample to be preferred over all of its lower-ranked alternatives. We empirically demonstrate the effectiveness of Diffusion-LPO across various tasks, including text-to-image generation, image editing, and personalized preference alignment. Diffusion-LPO consistently outperforms pairwise DPO baselines on visual quality and preference alignment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01540","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:51:41Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:51:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:51:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01454","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01454","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01454","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nilay","familyName":"Naharas","name":"Naharas, Nilay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dang","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Dang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nesihan","familyName":"Bulut","name":"Bulut, Nesihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadhossein","familyName":"Bateni","name":"Bateni, Mohammadhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vahab","familyName":"Mirrokni","name":"Mirrokni, Vahab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baharan","familyName":"Mirzasoleiman","name":"Mirzasoleiman, Baharan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data Selection for Fine-tuning Vision Language Models via Cross Modal Alignment Trajectories"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T20:47:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:10:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data-efficient learning aims to eliminate redundancy in large training datasets by training models on smaller subsets of the most informative examples. While data selection has been extensively explored for vision models and large language models (LLMs), it remains underexplored for Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs). Notably, none of existing methods can outperform random selection at different subset sizes. In this work, we propose the first principled method for data-efficient instruction tuning of LVLMs. We prove that examples with similar cross-modal attention matrices during instruction tuning have similar gradients. Thus, they influence model parameters in a similar manner and convey the same information to the model during training. Building on this insight, we propose XMAS, which clusters examples based on the trajectories of the top singular values of their attention matrices obtained from fine-tuning a small proxy LVLM. By sampling a balanced subset from these clusters, XMAS effectively removes redundancy in large-scale LVLM training data. Extensive experiments show that XMAS can discard 50% of the LLaVA-665k dataset and 85% of the Vision-Flan dataset while fully preserving performance of LLaVA-1.5-7B on 10 downstream benchmarks and speeding up its training by 1.2x. This is 30% more data reduction compared to the best baseline for LLaVA-665k. The project's website can be found at https://bigml-cs-ucla.github.io/XMAS-project-page/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, 10 figures, 5 tables, link: https://bigml-cs-ucla.github.io/XMAS-project-page/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01454","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:49:30Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:49:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:49:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01388","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01388","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01388","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arthur","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Arthur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyun","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Xiangyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Calliari","name":"Calliari, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong-Ki","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dong-Ki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shayegan","familyName":"Omidshafiei","name":"Omidshafiei, Shayegan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joydeep","familyName":"Biswas","name":"Biswas, Joydeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Agha","name":"Agha, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirreza","familyName":"Shaban","name":"Shaban, Amirreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VENTURA: Adapting Image Diffusion Models for Unified Task Conditioned Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T19:21:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:07:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robots must adapt to diverse human instructions and operate safely in unstructured, open-world environments. Recent Vision-Language models (VLMs) offer strong priors for grounding language and perception, but remain difficult to steer for navigation due to differences in action spaces and pretraining objectives that hamper transferability to robotics tasks. Towards addressing this, we introduce VENTURA, a vision-language navigation system that finetunes internet-pretrained image diffusion models for path planning. Instead of directly predicting low-level actions, VENTURA generates a path mask (i.e. a visual plan) in image space that captures fine-grained, context-aware navigation behaviors. A lightweight behavior-cloning policy grounds these visual plans into executable trajectories, yielding an interface that follows natural language instructions to generate diverse robot behaviors. To scale training, we supervise on path masks derived from self-supervised tracking models paired with VLM-augmented captions, avoiding manual pixel-level annotation or highly engineered data collection setups. In extensive real-world evaluations, VENTURA outperforms state-of-the-art foundation model baselines on object reaching, obstacle avoidance, and terrain preference tasks, improving success rates by 33% and reducing collisions by 54% across both seen and unseen scenarios. Notably, we find that VENTURA generalizes to unseen combinations of distinct tasks, revealing emergent compositional capabilities. Videos, code, and additional materials: https://venturapath.github.io"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 6 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01388","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:47:52Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:47:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:47:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01378","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01378","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01378","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kiwhan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Kiwhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaeyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jaeyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sitan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Sitan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sham","familyName":"Kakade","name":"Kakade, Sham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Sitzmann","name":"Sitzmann, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Selective Underfitting in Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T19:06:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:06:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have emerged as the principal paradigm for generative modeling across various domains. During training, they learn the score function, which in turn is used to generate samples at inference. They raise a basic yet unsolved question: which score do they actually learn? In principle, a diffusion model that matches the empirical score in the entire data space would simply reproduce the training data, failing to generate novel samples. Recent work addresses this question by arguing that diffusion models underfit the empirical score due to training-time inductive biases. In this work, we refine this perspective, introducing the notion of selective underfitting: instead of underfitting the score everywhere, better diffusion models more accurately approximate the score in certain regions of input space, while underfitting it in others. We characterize these regions and design empirical interventions to validate our perspective. Our results establish that selective underfitting is essential for understanding diffusion models, yielding new, testable insights into their generalization and generative performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01378","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:47:38Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:47:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:47:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01329","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01329","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01329","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huangjie","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Huangjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shansan","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Shansan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruixiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianrong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tianrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiatao","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Jiatao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyuan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Mingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Navdeep","familyName":"Jaitly","name":"Jaitly, Navdeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T18:00:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:03:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Standard discrete diffusion models treat all unobserved states identically by mapping them to an absorbing [MASK] token. This creates an 'information void' where semantic information that could be inferred from unmasked tokens is lost between denoising steps. We introduce Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion (CADD), a framework that augments the discrete state space with a paired diffusion in a continuous latent space. This yields graded, gradually corrupted states in which masked tokens are represented by noisy yet informative latent vectors rather than collapsed 'information voids'. At each reverse step, CADD may leverage the continuous latent as a semantic hint to guide discrete denoising. The design is clean and compatible with existing discrete diffusion training. At sampling time, the strength and choice of estimator for the continuous latent vector enables a controlled trade-off between mode-coverage (generating diverse outputs) and mode-seeking (generating contextually precise outputs) behaviors. Empirically, we demonstrate CADD improves generative quality over mask-based diffusion across text generation, image synthesis, and code modeling, with consistent gains on both qualitative and quantitative metrics against strong discrete baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01329","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:46:25Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:46:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:46:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01247","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01247","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01247","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Punit Kumar","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Punit Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nishant","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Nishant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akash","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Akash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunal","familyName":"Pasad","name":"Pasad, Kunal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khushi","familyName":"Soni","name":"Soni, Khushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manisha","familyName":"Jaishwal","name":"Jaishwal, Manisha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sriparna","familyName":"Saha","name":"Saha, Sriparna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syukron Abu Ishaq","familyName":"Alfarozi","name":"Alfarozi, Syukron Abu Ishaq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asres Temam","familyName":"Abagissa","name":"Abagissa, Asres Temam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kitsuchart","familyName":"Pasupa","name":"Pasupa, Kitsuchart","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiqin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Haiqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose G","familyName":"Moreno","name":"Moreno, Jose G","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Let's Play Across Cultures: A Large Multilingual, Multicultural Benchmark for Assessing Language Models' Understanding of Sports"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-24T09:06:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language Models (LMs) are primarily evaluated on globally popular sports, often overlooking regional and indigenous sporting traditions. To address this gap, we introduce \\textbf{\\textit{CultSportQA}}, a benchmark designed to assess LMs' understanding of traditional sports across 60 countries and 6 continents, encompassing four distinct cultural categories. The dataset features 33,000 multiple-choice questions (MCQs) across text and image modalities, each of which is categorized into three key types: history-based, rule-based, and scenario-based. To evaluate model performance, we employ zero-shot, few-shot, and chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting across a diverse set of Large Language Models (LLMs), Small Language Models (SLMs), and Multimodal Large Language Models (MLMs). By providing a comprehensive multilingual and multicultural sports benchmark, \\textbf{\\textit{CultSportQA}} establishes a new standard for assessing AI's ability to understand and reason about traditional sports."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"52 pages, 56 figures; appearing at EMNLP'25"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01247","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:24Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:44:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01236","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01236","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01236","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ismam Nur","familyName":"Swapnil","name":"Swapnil, Ismam Nur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aranya","familyName":"Saha","name":"Saha, Aranya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanvir Ahmed","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Tanvir Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Ariful","familyName":"Haque","name":"Haque, Mohammad Ariful","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GRPO++: Enhancing Dermatological Reasoning under Low Resource Settings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-23T18:32:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) show promise in medical image analysis, yet their capacity for structured reasoning in complex domains like dermatology is often limited by data scarcity and the high computational cost of advanced training techniques. To address these challenges, we introduce DermIQ-VLM, a VLM developed through a multi-stage, resource-efficient methodology designed to emulate a dermatologist's diagnostic process. Our primary contribution is a modified version of Grouped Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), called GRPO++, which stabilizes the powerful but data-intensive GRPO framework. Our proposed training pipeline first employs GRPO++ for reasoning-oriented disease recognition, followed by supervised fine-tuning for conversational ability. To mitigate factual errors introduced during this step, we then align the model using Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), leveraging a Knowledge Graph-based system as a scalable proxy for expert preference. A preliminary evaluation on a curated dermatological dataset demonstrates that our proposed methodology yields notable performance gains over standard fine-tuning approaches. These findings validate the potential of our pipeline as a feasible pathway for developing specialized, reliable VLMs in resource-constrained environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Will be submitted at IEEE JBHI"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01236","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:08Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:44:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":68,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-02T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-03T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28subjects.subject%3A%22cs.CV%22+OR+subjects.subject%3A%22cs.RO%22+OR+subjects.subject%3A%22cs.CL%22+OR+subjects.subject%3A%22cs.LG%22%29+AND+%28titles.title%3A%22vision+language%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22vision-language%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22VLA%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22world+model%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22world+models%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22multimodal+language%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22diffusion+model%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22diffusion+models%22+OR+titles.title%3A%22generative+foundation%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22vision+language%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22vision-language%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22VLA%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22world+model%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22world+models%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22multimodal+language%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22diffusion+model%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22diffusion+models%22+OR+descriptions.description%3A%22generative+foundation%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=1000\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1"}}